      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do treadmill mats reduce noise?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Partly. They reduce structure-borne vibration — the impact of your foot travelling through the frame into the floor — which is the noise that matters most to anyone below you. They do essentially nothing for airborne motor hum or belt noise. In a flat above a neighbour, expect a mat to take the edge off rather than to solve the problem.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need a mat if my treadmill is on carpet?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Arguably more than on a hard floor, which surprises most people. Carpet pulls fibres and dust into the motor housing where they trap heat, compresses unevenly so the frame never sits quite level, and generates static that is unhelpful for a controller board. A hard mat fixes all three and restores the transport wheels, which sink uselessly into pile.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What thickness treadmill mat do I need?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Density matters more than thickness. A half-inch of dense rubber outperforms an inch of soft foam for every job — floor protection, stopping creep and damping vibration. Compare weight per square foot rather than the thickness figure, and treat a large mat that is easy to carry one-handed as too light to do the vibration work.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can I use foam gym tiles under a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "You can, but it is the wrong choice. Interlocking foam and EVA tiles work well under free weights and compress permanently under a treadmill's sustained point loads. The feet sink in, the frame develops a slight rock that affects belt tracking, and the tiles are permanently dented. Dense rubber does not compress this way.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What size treadmill mat should I buy?",
