        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Buy <strong class="text-white">dense rubber, sized larger than the footprint</strong>, and
            compare weight per square foot rather than thickness. It protects the floor, stops the
            machine creeping, keeps carpet fibres out of the motor housing and damps vibration into
            the structure.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            What it will not do is quieten a motor or make a running treadmill acceptable above a light
            sleeper — for that, read the
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-apartments/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">apartment guide</a>
            before you buy the treadmill. And check a horse stall mat's price per square foot before
            paying for a fitness badge on the same rubber.
          </p>
        </div>
